WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.010
ED GLAESER: Urban proximity enables social interactions, some good and some bad.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.565
When those interactions are voluntary, like sharing a meal or getting a manicure,

00:00:08.565 --> 00:00:10.814
then the odds are that everyone expects to benefit.

00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:11.685
Otherwise they'd stay home.

00:00:11.685 --> 00:00:15.398
But some involuntary interactions, like a mugging or a murder,

00:00:15.398 --> 00:00:18.110
can do great harm to the whole web of urban life.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.930
Cities enable crime by making it easier for thieves or

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.350
bullies to have physical access to their victims.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.697
Cities can make it harder to catch criminals by increasing the number of

00:00:28.697 --> 00:00:29.266
suspects.

00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:33.017
In a classic British murder mystery set in, perhaps a country estate,

00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:35.891
the clever detective need only sort out the motives and

00:00:35.891 --> 00:00:38.790
whereabouts of a handful of potential perpetrators.

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:42.841
If Hercule Poirot had tried to solve a mystery in a teeming London slum,

00:00:42.841 --> 00:00:45.280
then the number of suspects would be vast

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.638
and finding the culprit might exceed the limits of even his numerous little

00:00:48.638 --> 00:00:49.230
gray cells.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.164
For millennia, cities had paid for

00:00:51.164 --> 00:00:54.960
law enforcement professionals to keep city streets safe.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.314
Indeed, the connection between police and cities is so deep,

00:00:58.314 --> 00:01:02.695
that the very word police has its roots in the Greek word "polis," which means city.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:06.130
Law itself is not a particularly urban thing.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:07.748
The English Common Law evolved for

00:01:07.748 --> 00:01:11.665
centuries focusing on the problems of protecting property rights for rural land.

00:01:11.665 --> 00:01:12.386
But paying for

00:01:12.386 --> 00:01:17.250
professional police only makes sense when they are protecting enough people.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:20.784
Police are essentially a fixed cost that needs to be shared, and

00:01:20.784 --> 00:01:23.269
sharing that cost requires sufficient scale.

00:01:23.269 --> 00:01:26.271
Preventive patrolling only makes sense when enough people are being

00:01:26.271 --> 00:01:29.410
protected to justify the cost of the patrolman's time.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:32.286
Ancient Athens had something of a police force.

00:01:32.286 --> 00:01:36.000
300 Syrian slaves, specialized archers, kept order.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.010
This was a pretty small number relative to Athens population.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.152
Which may have exceeded 300,000 by 430 BC, but

00:01:42.152 --> 00:01:46.269
the Ascidians were used more to prevent riots than to prosecute criminals.

00:01:46.269 --> 00:01:50.035
For normal crimes, Athenians had to press their own cases in front of 200 or

00:01:50.035 --> 00:01:51.230
more jurors.

00:01:51.230 --> 00:01:55.210
Augustus brought order to Roman chaos with two distinct forms of police,

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.790
three years after fire, damaged his own home.

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:02.652
The Princeps created a force of about 7,000 freed slaves in 6 AD to watch over

00:02:02.652 --> 00:02:04.290
the Roman night.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:08.910
They carried buckets to fight fires, but they also started fighting crime as well.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.190
Augustus also relied on the cohortes urbanae and his Praetorian guard,

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:18.030
who provided a more military force to stop riots and serious public disorders.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:21.100
When Rome fell, Europe returned to Agrarian ways and

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.850
organized public policing largely disappeared.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.448
There were fraternal organizations like the Holy Brotherhoods in Spain, or

00:02:27.448 --> 00:02:30.950
the Vehmic Courts in Germany, that came together to serve as vigilantes.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:35.420
Towns might also have night watchmen armed primarily with a rattle to raise the hew

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.449
and cry in the event of disturbance.

00:02:37.449 --> 00:02:40.892
The watchman might serve under a constable, but he was a local official,

00:02:40.892 --> 00:02:42.850
not a patrolling police man.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:46.652
Wealthy, private individuals, such as merchants or powerful clerics,

00:02:46.652 --> 00:02:48.655
might also hire their own armed guards.

00:02:48.655 --> 00:02:52.660
France is typically credited with being the birthplace of modern policing.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.600
King Francis I instituted a roving military police,

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:58.876
meant chiefly to check the plundering of his own soldiers.

00:02:58.876 --> 00:03:02.360
King Louis XIV centralized the French police force in 1666.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.590
And his police would be particularly focused on Paris.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.457
By the 18th century, Paris would have over 1,000 policemen.

00:03:08.457 --> 00:03:12.881
And, one police chief boasted to Louis the XV that whenever three people speak to one

00:03:12.881 --> 00:03:15.990
another on the street, one of these will be mine,

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.160
suggesting the repressive political role that police would also

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.897
play during the Bourbon monarchy.

00:03:20.897 --> 00:03:23.304
The English feared that oppressive power and so

00:03:23.304 --> 00:03:25.833
it wasn't until 1829 that Parliament passed

00:03:25.833 --> 00:03:30.150
the Metropolitan Police Act to provide a modern police force for London.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.820
English police have been known as both Bobbies and

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.755
Peelers after Sir Robert Peel who introduced the act.

00:03:35.755 --> 00:03:39.763
Fears of police abuse have long kept English police lightly armed carrying

00:03:39.763 --> 00:03:40.650
only clubs,

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.791
rather than the revolvers and automatic weapons that became the norm,

00:03:43.791 --> 00:03:46.475
both on the continent and in a more dangerous United States.

00:03:46.475 --> 00:03:50.695
America followed the English lead, in 1838 Boston instituted its police force,

00:03:50.695 --> 00:03:54.060
which was organized into the Boston Police Department in 1854.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.020
New York City organized its own police department in 1845.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.750
Just as in many developing world cities today,

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:05.670
America's 19th century police forces were consistently accused of corruption.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:06.984
Laws would be flouted and

00:04:06.984 --> 00:04:10.245
payments would be made to police officers to turn a blind eye.

00:04:10.245 --> 00:04:13.767
Some of the payments to individual policemen would work their way up

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:17.289
the system and end up enriching the coffers of political machines,

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:18.955
such as Tammany Hall.

00:04:18.954 --> 00:04:21.855
Reformers would consistently try to fix the system and

00:04:21.855 --> 00:04:23.689
some reforms did actually occur,

00:04:23.689 --> 00:04:29.077
especially when state legislatures were politically opposed to local leaders.

00:04:29.077 --> 00:04:33.286
In 1857, New York's overwhelmingly Republican state legislature decided

00:04:33.286 --> 00:04:36.458
to disband New York City's corrupt municipal police force,

00:04:36.458 --> 00:04:39.153
that served its Democratic mayor, Fernando Wood.

00:04:39.153 --> 00:04:43.134
And to replace it with a metropolitan police force that would be led by a former

00:04:43.134 --> 00:04:44.923
Whig Congressman.

00:04:44.923 --> 00:04:47.373
Wood initially refused to disband his municipal police,

00:04:47.373 --> 00:04:49.681
even after the State Supreme Court ruled against him.

00:04:49.681 --> 00:04:53.187
The metropolitan police then attempted to take over City Hall, but

00:04:53.187 --> 00:04:57.711
the municipals fought back in what became know as the Great Police Riot.

00:04:57.711 --> 00:04:59.541
The metropolitan's were defeated and

00:04:59.541 --> 00:05:02.874
they retreated to the Office of the City Recorder to tend their wounds.

00:05:02.874 --> 00:05:06.992
Eventually, the seventh regiment stepped in and would surrender temporarily, but

00:05:06.992 --> 00:05:09.800
the rivalry between the two forces continued.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.530
And New York suffered a crime spree,

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.870
culminating in the deadly gang war known as the Dead Rabbits Riot.

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:16.635
After another adverse court ruling,

00:05:16.635 --> 00:05:19.289
Wood eventually disbanded his municipal police force.

00:05:19.289 --> 00:05:22.384
But the corruption in New York City's police department continued

00:05:22.384 --> 00:05:24.723
during the 19th century heyday of Tammany Hall.

00:05:24.723 --> 00:05:28.964
In 1894 republican state legislators again turn their gaze upon the democratically

00:05:28.964 --> 00:05:30.550
controlled police force.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:35.400
At an inquiry, the Lexow Committee exposed the enormous irregularities.

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.960
Alexander Williams, known as Clubber because of his strong arm tactics,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.227
was a particular target of the investigation.

00:05:41.227 --> 00:05:45.047
When Williams was appointed to the city's West 13th Street Station in 1876,

00:05:45.047 --> 00:05:46.607
he relished the opportunities for

00:05:46.607 --> 00:05:49.690
extracting bribes from the abundant saloons and brothels nearby.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.423
He famously said, "I've been having chuck steak ever since I've been on the force,

00:05:53.423 --> 00:05:55.960
and now I'm gonna have a bit of tenderloin."

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.533
Henceforth, the neighborhood would be known as the Tenderloin District.

00:05:59.533 --> 00:06:02.141
When the Lexow Commission wondered how Williams could

00:06:02.141 --> 00:06:05.995
afford his Cos Cob estate and his yacht, Clubber explained that he had earned his

00:06:05.995 --> 00:06:08.449
great wealth by speculating in Tokyo real estate.

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.760
The Lexow Committee's revelations lead to the election of a reforming mayor,

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:13.480
William Strong,

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.230
who appointed the young Teddy Roosevelt to be a police commissioner.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:20.162
Roosevelt started appointing officers based on merit, not political patronage, and

00:06:20.162 --> 00:06:23.360
he prowled the streets at night to see if his policemen were awake and

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.780
doing their jobs.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:28.560
His assiduous enforcement of the city's anti-alcohol blue laws actually helped

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.185
Strong lose his re-election bid.

00:06:30.185 --> 00:06:33.733
There were just some laws that New Yorkers didn't want to have enforced.

00:06:33.733 --> 00:06:36.340
The Regressive Era enacted numerous police reforms,

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:39.235
officers were increasingly appointed based on exams.

00:06:39.235 --> 00:06:42.630
Professionalization and police rotation tried to break the ties between policemen

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.070
and local businesses that enabled corruption.

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:49.125
By the 1960s citizens became increasingly intolerant of police brutality.

00:06:49.125 --> 00:06:53.770
And America's cops were criticized for doing too much, and too little.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:57.427
As crime rates surged during the 1970's many observers thought that social

00:06:57.427 --> 00:06:59.477
policies, not police, were the solution.

00:06:59.477 --> 00:07:02.867
But crime couldn't be stopped with public largess alone, and so

00:07:02.867 --> 00:07:07.220
under Rudy Giuliani New York again embraced a strong police force.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.055
The cops began to target high crime areas and

00:07:09.055 --> 00:07:11.395
followed a strategy known as "broken windows policing."

00:07:11.395 --> 00:07:14.510
They targeted small quality of life crimes.

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:16.183
The city did become much safer and

00:07:16.183 --> 00:07:20.329
New York now has a police force that is both effective and generally quite honest.

00:07:20.329 --> 00:07:24.737
The pleasures of the city can now be enjoyed without fear both day and night.

00:07:24.737 --> 00:07:28.590
The downside of that success is that too many have been locked up.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.914
Thousands were imprisoned for minor offenses, and

00:07:30.914 --> 00:07:34.260
ordinary New Yorkers chafed under strong arm tactics like stop and frisk.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.808
The task of making city streets safe is only half finished.

00:07:37.808 --> 00:07:40.508
Crime's a fraction of what it was, but we have not yet

00:07:40.508 --> 00:07:44.360
figured out how to fight crime without imprisoning thousands of young men.

